  "title": "Mib1 induces a neuronal sterol program that Notch ligands and receptors fail to mimic",
  "status": "grounded",
  "rejection_reason": null,
  "exact_observed_result": "Mib1 perturbation induced a recurrent all-up sterol/cholesterol program: in 151 TH, Insig1 logFC 1.52772855758667 FDR 1.2485262063074146e-19, Dhcr24 1.1180424690246582 FDR 4.973949083479575e-17, Fdft1 1.6080328226089478 FDR 4.973949083479575e-17, Ldlr 2.095721483230591 FDR 1.7299382480231483e-15, and Hmgcr 0.9627998471260071 FDR 9.825108272472224e-13. Dll1, Notch1-4, Adam10, and Psen1 were weak or sterol-silent in matched analyses.",
  "biological_interpretation": "The dominant Mib1 loss signature in adult neurons is a SREBP/cholesterol response rather than a canonical Notch-target transcript response, although a sender-cell Notch defect remains biologically plausible and unmeasured.",
